[{:seon.task/name "files-namespaces-and-symbols-are-plain-markdown"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-10-21 (DECISIONS: files, namespaces and symbols are plain Markdown, read by the one reader): no syntax beyond Markdown; `seon.ref` reads a backticked span or a link and knows what it names. Two gaps against that today: (1) a backticked dotted name that names a namespace (`seon.ref`) is dropped: `seon.ref/span-candidate` keeps only spans holding a `/`. Port v1's rule (`~/src/seon/src/seon/ref.clj` `span-candidate`: a dotted simple symbol is a resolve-only name, kept when it names a namespace on the db or loaded) so `seon.ref/references` answers it and the context shows a namespace at its depth (summary its docstring's first sentence, doc its docstring and publics). (2) A cited file outside the stored wiki (`bin/root`, `src/seon/db.clj`, `reference-code/<project>/README.md#x`) is a file reference resolved on demand: read and parsed when the context is built (`seon.ref/parse` and `seon.ref/shown` for Markdown, the text whole at code depth for any file), never stored (`seon.docs` stores only `docs/`). For `reference-code/`, the reference records the submodule's pinned revision when it resolves (looked up from git, never written by the author), as v1's `:seon.ref/pin`. Extend `seon.context/current-state`'s `:sections` (or one neighbouring key, one rule) rather than a second mechanism; write the rule into `docs/references.md`. Landed (lane FILEREF): `seon.ref/namespace-view` and `seon.ref/file-view` (one `shown` rule, a pin for `reference-code/`), all three kinds in `:sections` via `seon.context/cited-view`; `:seon.ref/name` also accepts a simple symbol; a `path:N` citation is not shown; a non-Markdown file at summary or doc shows its address and line count only; rule in docs/references.md#files-and-namespaces-on-demand."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ref-test/a-namespace-is-read-as-a-namespace
                      seon.context-test/a-cited-file-is-read-on-demand}}
